-- title:
--   Euler inequality for a pair of permutations (genus of a combinatorial hypermap is nonnegative)
-- statement:
--   Let $\alpha$ be a finite type and let $\sigma, \theta$ be permutations of $\alpha$ (`Equiv.Perm α`). For a permutation $\pi$, write $c(\pi)$ for its number of cycles, **fixed points included**: the number of classes of the relation "$x$ and $y$ lie in the same cycle of $\pi$" (Mathlib's `Equiv.Perm.SameCycle.setoid π`), counted with `Nat.card`. Write $k(\sigma, \theta)$ for the number of classes of the join $\texttt{SameCycle.setoid}\ \sigma \sqcup \texttt{SameCycle.setoid}\ \theta$, the smallest equivalence relation containing both. These are the orbits of the subgroup generated by $\sigma$ and $\theta$. Then
--   $$c(\sigma) + c(\theta) + c(\sigma\theta) \;\le\; |\alpha| + 2\,k(\sigma, \theta),$$
--   where $|\alpha|$ is `Nat.card α` and $\sigma\theta$ is the composite $\sigma * \theta$ (first $\theta$, then $\sigma$).
--
--   In the language of combinatorial (hyper)maps, $(\sigma, \theta, (\sigma\theta)^{-1})$ describes an oriented surface embedding on each orbit. The left side counts its vertices, edges and faces, so the inequality says the Euler characteristic of each connected piece is at most $2$, that is, its genus is nonnegative. The statement is purely combinatorial: no surface or topological statement is assumed or asserted. For $\theta$ a fixed-point-free involution it is Euler's inequality $V - E + F \le 2k$ for a combinatorial map, since then $c(\theta) = |\alpha|/2$ counts edges.
--
--   Lean: `OAI.Kervaire.perm_euler_inequality`. It restates OpenAI's lemma `OAI.KervaireRibbon.euler_inequality` (in `lean/OAI/GroupTheory/Kervaire/Components.lean`), which counts cycles with its own definitions `numCycles` and `classes`, with the counts written as `Nat.card` of Mathlib quotients and the type only `Finite`. The statement uses Mathlib alone and imports no definitions bundle.
-- source:
--   Classical: the Euler characteristic inequality for combinatorial maps and hypermaps given by two permutations of a finite set. Lean proof: OpenAI, The Kervaire theorem for groups, OpenAI Math Release, September 24, 2026, https://github.com/openai/math (result family 256; paper: https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026.pdf; Apache License 2.0); Lean: https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/lean/OAI/GroupTheory/Kervaire/Components.lean (declaration OAI.KervaireRibbon.euler_inequality, restated with Mathlib-only notions)

import Mathlib

theorem OAI.Kervaire.perm_euler_inequality {α : Type*} [Finite α] (σ θ : Equiv.Perm α) :
    Nat.card (Quotient (Equiv.Perm.SameCycle.setoid σ)) +
        Nat.card (Quotient (Equiv.Perm.SameCycle.setoid θ)) +
        Nat.card (Quotient (Equiv.Perm.SameCycle.setoid (σ * θ))) ≤
      Nat.card α + 2 * Nat.card (Quotient
        (Equiv.Perm.SameCycle.setoid σ ⊔ Equiv.Perm.SameCycle.setoid θ)) := by
  sorry
